          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My knees hurt most days and I have been told to lose weight. Is joint pain ever urgent, and what does guidance describe as core treatment?",
          "answer": "Joint pain is usually not an emergency, but some patterns need prompt assessment. The triage check for your description returned a routine result, which does not rule out a serious cause. If your knee becomes hot, very swollen and sharply painful, especially with fever, get urgent medical assessment. The Osteoarthritis summary (condition-osteoarthritis) says this pattern suggests infection or crystal arthritis, not a typical osteoarthritis flare. The same summary also flags sudden locking or giving way of a joint, and new nerve symptoms with back pain, as reasons for prompt assessment. Morning stiffness that lasts a long time, or several small joints hurting on both sides, may point to a different kind of arthritis and is worth raising with a clinician.\n\nFor core treatment, the osteoarthritis guideline (guideline-osteoarthritis-management) describes exercise as the foundation. Exercise is paired with weight management where body weight is above the healthy range. The guideline says a 5 to 10 percent weight reduction gives clinically meaningful improvement, and that exercise and weight loss together work better than either alone. Education, pacing, suitable footwear, walking aids where they help, and heat or cold for relief are also described as supporting measures. The guideline describes medicines as add-ons to exercise, not replacements for it. Joint replacement is considered only when pain and limited movement continue despite these non-surgical measures.\n\nThe exercise summary (lifestyle-osteoarthritis-activity) notes that water-based exercise, cycling and walking are generally well tolerated. It also says that during a flare, it helps to reduce intensity for a few days rather than stop.\n\nThe retrieved material does not cover your specific knee, your diagnosis, or which exercises or medicines suit you. A clinician who knows your history can help with those questions.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
